{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I understand you came in with abdominal pain again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, same pain—epigastric, radiating to my back. Feels just like my usual flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You haven’t been able to eat for two days and feel nauseated?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Exactly. I’ve been on IV fluids in the ER. Can I get something for the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll continue IV hydromorphone as needed. We’ve reviewed your labs—lipase is 111, which is mildly elevated but not high enough to confirm pancreatitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But it’s always been like this—my lipase never goes super high. Doesn’t mean it’s not pancreatitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But your liver tests and ultrasound are normal. No gallstones, no duct dilation. The pancreas looks unremarkable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had four ERCPs before. The last one showed sphincter issues. Why won’t they fix it again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The GI team reviewed your history. They don’t think sphincter of Oddi dysfunction is a valid diagnosis anymore. There’s no objective evidence it’s causing your pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I’ve had multiple sphincterotomies. They helped before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. But repeated procedures haven’t stopped the admissions. You’ve been here over 50 times in ten years. We need to think differently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So you’re saying it’s all in my head?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, the pain is real. But it may not be from pancreatitis. Chronic pain can change how the gut works—called narcotic bowel or motility disorder."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Motility disorder? What does that mean?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It means your intestines aren’t moving food through normally. Pain, nausea, constipation—can all be part of it. Especially with long-term narcotic use."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I need the pain meds. The pain is unbearable without them."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We know. That’s why we’re suggesting a pain management referral. They help reduce reliance on narcotics and improve function."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I don’t want to go. I’ve tried before. It didn’t help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s okay. We’ll respect your decision. But we do recommend a bowel regimen. We’ve started Miralax for constipation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I already take that sometimes. But when I’m in pain, I can’t eat, so I don’t poop."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right. So improving gut motility might help break that cycle. We also tried Dexilant today for stomach acid, but you said it gave you a headache?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, right after I took it. I get migraines from PPIs. I stick with ranitidine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2155-12-29 16:43:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. We’ll keep you on high-dose ranitidine. No more PPIs unless you’re willing to try another time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your ultrasound from yesterday was normal—no gallstones, no pancreas issues. That supports our thinking."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I still hurt. Doesn’t the pain mean something’s wrong?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It does. But sometimes pain persists even when scans look normal. That’s common in chronic pain syndromes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s the plan? Just send me home with pain meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep treating symptoms. But we’re also focusing on long-term strategies—like improving gut motility and reducing narcotic dependence."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I just want the pain to stop. I can’t keep living like this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2155-12-30 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We hear you. A social worker will meet with you to offer support. And we encourage you to consider the pain clinic in the future."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing better today. You had some toast and tolerated it. That’s progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the nausea is less. But I still have a dull ache."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s expected. We’re tapering your IV pain meds. You’re now on oral hydromorphone as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I go home soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly by tomorrow or the next day. We want to make sure you can eat and manage pain with oral meds first."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2155-12-31 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do whatever I need to. I just want to be out of here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You had a full meal yesterday and no vomiting. Your pain is much improved. We think you’re ready for discharge soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. But what about the ERCP I was supposed to have?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s still scheduled as an outpatient. But the team doesn’t expect it to change your symptoms, given the lack of findings."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I guess I’ll go, just in case. My last one helped for a few months."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll support your choice. But we want you to know—most experts now doubt sphincter of Oddi dysfunction is real."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s frustrating. You’re saying everything I’ve been treated for isn’t real?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at all. Your pain is real. But the cause may not be structural. It could be how your nerves and gut interact—called a functional disorder."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what helps that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Things like stress management, meditation, yoga. There are mind-body programs that help retrain how the brain processes pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve never tried that. Seems… different."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It is. But many patients find it helpful alongside medication. We can give you info if you’re interested."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2156-01-01 10:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Maybe later. Right now, I just want to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re cleared to go home today. You’ve been off IV meds, eating well, and your pain is minimal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. What should I do if the pain comes back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Use the oral hydromorphone as needed, stay hydrated, and try to eat small meals. If it’s severe or you can’t keep food down, come back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the Miralax?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue it twice daily as needed. It helps keep your bowels moving, which may reduce pain episodes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ll try."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your follow-up with GI is scheduled. They’ll discuss the ERCP. But we encourage you to also consider non-medication approaches long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll think about it. Thank you for listening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out if you need help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One more thing—can I drink coffee? I had some the other day when I was hungry."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "In moderation, yes. But caffeine can worsen anxiety and gut motility. Try to limit it if possible."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try. It’s hard to give up everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We know. Small changes over time can make a big difference. You’re not alone in this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. I’ll take that with me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One last question—what’s the official diagnosis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re calling it a motility bowel disorder causing episodic abdominal pain. It means your gut isn’t moving properly, leading to pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. Not pancreatitis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2156-01-03 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not based on your labs and scans. But we’ll keep listening and adjusting as needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2155-12-29 15:44:00",
    "admission_end": "2156-01-03 19:55:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a long history of recurrent abdominal pain and multiple prior hospitalizations was admitted for epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and inability to tolerate oral intake. Labs showed only a mildly elevated lipase (111), and imaging revealed a normal ultrasound with no biliary or pancreatic abnormalities. The GI team found no evidence of acute pancreatitis or structural cause, and questioned the validity of prior sphincter of Oddi dysfunction diagnoses. The hospital course focused on symptom management with IV fluids, pain control, and antiemetics, followed by gradual advancement to oral intake. A motility bowel disorder was considered the likely cause of her episodic pain, possibly related to chronic narcotic use. She declined chronic pain referral but tolerated a regular diet before discharge. She was discharged home with instructions to continue ranitidine, Miralax, and home psychiatric medications, with follow-up for a planned outpatient ERCP and encouragement to consider non-pharmacologic pain management strategies.",
    "problems": [
      "Other functional disorders of intestine",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified",
      "Posttraumatic stress disorder",
      "Unspecified hearing loss",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified"
    ]
  }
}
